  "interpretation": "<p data-start=\"160\" data-end=\"689\">The <strong data-start=\"164\" data-end=\"197\">24-hour urinary chloride test</strong> measures the amount of chloride excreted in urine over a full day. Chloride is a major electrolyte involved in maintaining acid-base balance, fluid balance, and proper muscle and nerve function. This test is often used to evaluate the cause of <strong data-start=\"442\" data-end=\"465\">metabolic alkalosis</strong>, investigate <strong data-start=\"479\" data-end=\"505\">electrolyte imbalances</strong>, or assess <strong data-start=\"517\" data-end=\"556\">renal (kidney) handling of chloride</strong>, especially in conditions involving prolonged vomiting, diuretic use, or suspected mineralocorticoid excess (e.g., Conn's syndrome).</p>\n<p data-start=\"742\" data-end=\"789\">High levels of urinary chloride may indicate:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"790\" data-end=\"1185\">\n<li data-start=\"790\" data-end=\"850\">\n<p data-start=\"792\" data-end=\"850\"><strong data-start=\"792\" data-end=\"808\">Diuretic use</strong> (especially loop or thiazide diuretics)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"851\" data-end=\"930\">\n<p data-start=\"853\" data-end=\"930\"><strong data-start=\"853\" data-end=\"881\">Salt-wasting nephropathy</strong> (kidney loses salt due to tubular dysfunction)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"931\" data-end=\"1019\">\n<p data-start=\"933\" data-end=\"1019\"><strong data-start=\"933\" data-end=\"953\">Chronic vomiting</strong> (if the body compensates by increased renal chloride excretion)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1020\" data-end=\"1087\">\n<p data-start=\"1022\" data-end=\"1087\"><strong data-start=\"1022\" data-end=\"1050\">Mineralocorticoid excess</strong> (e.g., primary hyperaldosteronism)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1088\" data-end=\"1185\">\n<p data-start=\"1090\" data-end=\"1185\"><strong data-start=\"1090\" data-end=\"1122\">Bartter or Gitelman syndrome</strong> (genetic tubulopathies leading to loss of chloride in urine)</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"1187\" data-end=\"1395\">Elevated chloride in urine often suggests that the kidneys are actively excreting chloride despite systemic depletion, which helps differentiate <strong data-start=\"1332\" data-end=\"1394\">chloride-responsive vs. non-responsive metabolic alkalosis</strong>.</p>\n<p data-start=\"1445\" data-end=\"1488\">Low urinary chloride levels may indicate:</p>\n<ul data-start=\"1489\" data-end=\"1829\">\n<li data-start=\"1489\" data-end=\"1600\">\n<p data-start=\"1491\" data-end=\"1600\"><strong data-start=\"1491\" data-end=\"1534\">Chloride-responsive metabolic alkalosis</strong> (e.g., from vomiting, nasogastric suction, or volume depletion)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1601\" data-end=\"1694\">\n<p data-start=\"1603\" data-end=\"1694\"><strong data-start=\"1603\" data-end=\"1638\">Dehydration or volume depletion</strong>, where the kidney retains chloride to conserve volume</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1695\" data-end=\"1765\">\n<p data-start=\"1697\" data-end=\"1765\"><strong data-start=\"1697\" data-end=\"1716\">Cystic fibrosis</strong> (can sometimes show altered chloride handling)</p>\n</li>\n<li data-start=\"1766\" data-end=\"1829\">\n<p data-start=\"1768\" data-end=\"1829\"><strong data-start=\"1768\" data-end=\"1792\">Overuse of laxatives</strong> or poor dietary intake of chloride</p>\n</li>\n</ul>\n<p data-start=\"1831\" data-end=\"2007\">Low levels generally suggest the body is trying to <strong data-start=\"1882\" data-end=\"1903\">conserve chloride</strong>, typically in response to volume depletion or loss through non-renal routes (e.g., gastric fluid loss).</p>",
